---

<LessonLayout title="L2 — Your First Agent in Node.js"
>

  <LessonHeader
    num="L02"
    title="Your First Agent in Node.js"
    subtitle="Implement the loop from scratch · No framework · Just the Anthropic SDK"
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      You'll build a command-line agent that answers questions requiring real tool use.
      Give it a task like <em>"What files are in my current directory and how many are there?"</em>
      — it calls a shell command, observes the result, and returns a human-readable answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      No LangChain, no Vercel AI SDK. Just the <strong>Anthropic SDK</strong> and the loop
      from Lesson 1 — so you know exactly what every framework does under the hood.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Step 1 — Project setup</h2>

    <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`
mkdir my-first-agent && cd my-first-agent
npm init -y
npm install @anthropic-ai/sdk
touch agent.mjs
    `} />

    <p>
      We use <code>.mjs</code> for top-level <code>await</code> without extra config.
      Add your API key in a <code>.env</code> file and add it to <code>.gitignore</code>:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`
echo "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-..." > .env
echo ".env" >> .gitignore
    `} />

    <LessonCallout variant="info">
      <p>Get a key at <strong>console.anthropic.com</strong>. Haiku is the cheapest model — we use it here.</p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Step 2 — The agent loop (annotated)</h2>

    <p>Every line of code below maps to one of these four steps:</p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  A["1 · SEND\ngoal + tools\n→ LLM"]:::step --> B{"stop_reason?"}:::decision
  B -->|end_turn| C["4 · RETURN\nanswer"]:::done
  B -->|tool_use| D["2 · EXECUTE\ntool call"]:::step
  D --> E["3 · APPEND\ntool_result\nto messages"]:::step
  E --> A

  classDef step     fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef decision fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done     fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <CodeBlock lang="javascript" label="agent.mjs" code={`
import Anthropic from '@anthropic-ai/sdk';
import { execSync } from 'child_process';

const client = new Anthropic({ apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY });

// ── 1. DEFINE TOOLS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A tool is a JSON schema + a handler function.
// The LLM reads the schema to decide when and how to call the tool.

const tools = [
  {
    name: 'run_shell',
    description:
      'Run a safe, read-only shell command and return its output. ' +
      'Use for listing files, checking environment, reading file contents.',
    input_schema: {
      type: 'object',
      properties: {
        command: {
          type: 'string',
          description: 'The shell command to run (read-only operations only)',
        },
      },
      required: ['command'],
    },
  },
];

// ── 2. TOOL HANDLER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This is the code that actually executes when the LLM calls a tool.
// Return strings — never throw — so the LLM can recover from errors.

function executeTool(name, input) {
  if (name === 'run_shell') {
    try {
      return execSync(input.command, { encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 5000 });
    } catch (err) {
      return \`Error: \${err.message}\`;
    }
  }
  return 'Unknown tool';
}

// ── 3. THE AGENT LOOP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This is the core. Exactly the 4-step loop from Lesson 1.

async function runAgent(userMessage) {
  console.log(\`\\n🤖 Task: \${userMessage}\\n\`);

  // The messages array IS the agent's memory.
  // Every turn — user message, assistant response, tool result — appends here.
  const messages = [{ role: 'user', content: userMessage }];
  const MAX_TURNS = 10;
  let turns = 0;

  while (turns < MAX_TURNS) {
    turns++;
    // STEP 1: Send current state to the LLM
    const response = await client.messages.create({
      model: 'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001',
      max_tokens: 1024,
      tools,
      messages,
    });

    // STEP 2: Check what the LLM returned
    if (response.stop_reason === 'end_turn') {
      const text = response.content
        .filter(b => b.type === 'text')
        .map(b => b.text)
        .join('');
      console.log(\`\\n✅ Answer:\\n\${text}\\n\`);
      return text;
    }

    if (response.stop_reason === 'tool_use') {
      // Append the assistant's decision before executing — the LLM needs to see
      // its own tool call on the next iteration to understand the context.
      messages.push({ role: 'assistant', content: response.content });

      // STEP 3: Execute each tool call and collect results
      const toolResults = response.content
        .filter(b => b.type === 'tool_use')
        .map(toolCall => {
          console.log(\`  🔧 Tool: \${toolCall.name} → \${JSON.stringify(toolCall.input)}\`);
          const result = executeTool(toolCall.name, toolCall.input);
          console.log(\`  📋 Result: \${result.slice(0, 120)}...\`);
          return { type: 'tool_result', tool_use_id: toolCall.id, content: result };
        });

      // Append tool results — the LLM reads these on the next iteration
      messages.push({ role: 'user', content: toolResults });

      // STEP 4: Loop — go back to top and call the LLM again
    }
  }
  throw new Error('Agent exceeded ' + MAX_TURNS + ' turns without completing.');
}

// ── 4. RUN IT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
await runAgent('List the files in the current directory and tell me how many there are.');
    `} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Step 3 — Run it</h2>

    <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`node --env-file=.env agent.mjs`} />

    <p>You should see something like:</p>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="output" code={`
🤖 Task: List the files in the current directory and tell me how many there are.

  🔧 Tool: run_shell → {"command":"ls -la"}
  📋 Result: total 32
drwxr-xr-x  4 user staff  128 Jun 14 ...

✅ Answer:
The current directory contains 4 items:
- agent.mjs
- node_modules/
- package.json
- package-lock.json
    `} />

    <p>
      What just happened: the LLM decided to call <code>run_shell</code>, your code executed it,
      the result was fed back in, and the LLM synthesised a human-readable answer. That is the
      full agent loop — you built it from scratch.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What frameworks actually do</h2>

    <p>
      Every framework — LangChain, Vercel AI SDK, LangGraph — wraps this exact same pattern. They add:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Convenience for defining and registering tools</li>
      <li>Built-in error handling and retries</li>
      <li>Streaming support</li>
      <li>Graph-based control flow for multi-agent setups</li>
      <li>Observability hooks</li>
    </ul>
    <p>None of them change the core loop. When something breaks inside a framework, you now know where to look.</p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Stretch — try a multi-step task</h2>

    <p>Change the last line to a task requiring multiple tool calls:</p>

    <CodeBlock lang="javascript" label="agent.mjs" code={`
await runAgent(
  'Read the contents of package.json and tell me what version of Node this project expects.'
);
    `} />

    <p>
      Watch the loop iterate more than once. The LLM will call <code>ls</code>, then
      <code>cat package.json</code>, then synthesise an answer from both results.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="The mental model">
      <p>
        An agent is a <code>while</code> loop around an LLM call. The loop exits when
        <code>stop_reason === 'end_turn'</code>. The <code>messages</code> array is the only
        memory the stateless LLM has — every turn must be appended, including the LLM's own
        tool-call decisions.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Quick-check quiz</h2>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'What does stop_reason: "tool_use" mean in the agent loop?',
        options: [
          'The LLM wants to call a tool before it can answer',
          'The agent has finished and the answer is ready',
          'A tool threw an error and the loop should stop',
          'The context window is full',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'tool_use = LLM needs more information from a tool. Your code must execute it and loop again. end_turn = done, return the answer.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Why append the assistant response to messages before executing the tool?',
        options: [
          'So the LLM can see its own decision on the next iteration',
          'Because the SDK requires it for request validation',
          'To reduce token count on the next call',
          'So the tool handler can read what the LLM said',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'The messages array is the agent\'s memory. The LLM needs to see its own tool-call request so the tool_result on the next turn makes sense.',
      },
      {
        q:       'The LLM never directly executes tools. Who does?',
        options: [
          'Your code — the executeTool function',
          'The Anthropic cloud infrastructure',
          'A separate tool-executor LLM',
          'The Node.js event loop automatically',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'The LLM outputs intent only — a tool name and arguments. Your executeTool function runs the actual shell command, DB query, etc.',
      },
      {
        q:       'The messages array grows every loop iteration. What is its role?',
        options: [
          "The agent's memory — the LLM reads all of it on every call",
          'A debug log — it has no effect on the LLM responses',
          'Used only for error reporting',
          'Cleared after each tool call to save tokens',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'The LLM is stateless. The messages array is the only memory it has. It sees the full array on every API call — that\'s how it knows what it already tried.',
      },
    ]} />

  </div>

  <DeepDive
    sources="l2_2.5, l2_2.6"
    tensions={[
      { a: 'Custom ReAct loop',       b: 'Framework abstraction' },
      { a: 'System prompt verbosity', b: 'Token cost per call' },
      { a: 'Tight loop control',      b: 'Agent flexibility' },
      { a: 'Low max_turns (safe)',     b: 'High max_turns (capable)' },
    ]}
    questions={[
      {
        q:      'Your agent enters a loop calling the same tool with slightly different arguments without making progress. How do you detect and break it?',
        hint:   'Detect repetition, not just max_turns. Same tool + similar arguments is a stuck signal.',
        answer: '<p>Maintain a <code>tool_call_history</code>. After each call append <code>(tool_name, hash_of_args)</code>. If the last 2 entries have the same tool and similar args hash — inject a message: <em>"You have called [tool] multiple times without new information. Try a different approach or provide a best-effort answer."</em></p><p>If still stuck after 2 more turns: force termination and return a partial result with explanation. Track "stuck detected" as a metric — a high rate signals the tools or prompt need redesign.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'Why does the full message history need to be sent to every LLM call? What breaks if you only send the latest message?',
        hint:   'LLMs are stateless. The history IS the state.',
        answer: '<p>LLMs are completely stateless between API calls. If you send only the latest message, the LLM sees no original task, no previous tool results, no available tools (unless re-injected). It hallucinates context or gives a nonsensical response.</p><p>Every call must include: <code>[system, user_msg, assistant_turn_1, tool_result_1, assistant_turn_2, ...]</code>. This costs tokens, but there is no alternative. Optimise by compressing old turns — not eliminating them.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'What are the 4 essential elements of an effective agent system prompt, and what goes wrong if each is missing?',
        hint:   'Each element serves a specific purpose. What does the LLM do without it?',
        answer: '<p><strong>1. Loop format instructions</strong> ("Think → Act → Observe"): without this, the LLM doesn\'t know to alternate thinking and acting.</p><p><strong>2. Available tools list with examples</strong>: without this, the LLM may hallucinate tool calls or call non-existent tools.</p><p><strong>3. Termination instruction</strong> ("After each tool call, wait for the result"): without this, the LLM generates both the call and a hallucinated result without waiting.</p><p><strong>4. A worked example session</strong>: without this, the LLM understands abstractly but formats output incorrectly. The example is the template the LLM replicates.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'The loop hits max_turns on every complex query. What changes do you make — to the system prompt, tools, or max_turns?',
        hint:   'Diagnose first: are queries genuinely complex, or is the agent being inefficient?',
        answer: '<p>Log the full turn sequence for 10 failing runs. Common patterns:</p><p><strong>Agent re-verifies everything</strong> → add to prompt: "Trust your tool results. Do not re-verify already-retrieved information."</p><p><strong>Agent can\'t find specific info</strong> → add: "If you cannot find X after 2 searches, provide a best-effort answer stating the limitation."</p><p><strong>Queries are genuinely complex</strong> → add a complexity classifier; set <code>max_turns=20</code> for complex, <code>max_turns=5</code> for simple.</p><p>Fix root causes — redesign tools to return richer data in fewer calls rather than raising max_turns as a band-aid.</p>',
      },
    ]}
    pitfalls={[
      'Message history growing without bounds — after 15 cycles you may have 45+ messages filling the context window. Implement history trimming or summarisation for long-running agents.',
      'No distinction between recoverable and terminal tool failures — <code>"API key invalid"</code> is terminal; <code>"429 rate limited"</code> is recoverable. Without the distinction, the agent wastes turns retrying a terminal error.',
      'Silent max_turns termination — the loop hits the limit and returns <code>undefined</code> with no explanation. Always return the best available partial result plus an explicit message when terminating early. Log a WARNING.',
      'Return error strings from tools, never throw — throwing breaks the message conversation flow. The LLM cannot observe the error and adjust; it just crashes the loop.',
    ]}
  />

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>
